From 8ae41a6684604e3caba5d4e5fe0edba04bd3f3a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Xun2202 <xun2202@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 05:11:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] android: keep the app process alive while web-source caches
 download
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Web-source (HTTP / m3u8) caches are downloaded by KtorHttpDownloader
inside the app process. Only the BT engine has a foreground service, and
that one lives in :torrent_service, so an HTTP cache has no notification
and nothing stops the system from freezing the app process once it goes
to the background. On HarmonyOS 卓易通 the freeze is immediate: a log
from a device shows all segment requests stalling the moment the app is
backgrounded and timing out 22 minutes later when it was reopened.

Add HttpCacheService, a dataSync foreground service in the app process
that runs while any in-process cache is IN_PROGRESS, shows a progress
notification with a "pause all" action, and honours the Android 15
dataSync timeout. HttpCacheServiceController starts and stops it from
MediaDownloadManager snapshots. ServiceNotification gains notificationId
/ channelId / stop-intent parameters so both services can share it; the
BT service keeps the previous values via defaults. Mirrors upstream
main's PikPakCacheService, which solves the same problem for PikPak.
